diff --git a/docs/storage-usage.md b/docs/storage-usage.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..c51b984
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/storage-usage.md
@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+# Storage usage
+
+`Account#bytes_used` and `Board#bytes_used` return integer byte counts. They count
+card images and files embedded in board public descriptions, card descriptions,
+and comment bodies. Avatars, exports, import archives, unattached direct uploads,
+and generated previews/variants are excluded. Each attachment reference counts;
+attaching the same blob twice counts it twice.
+
+Counts are eventually consistent: the recurring `reconcile_storage_usage` task
+dispatches pending adjustments every minute. Run the Solid Queue scheduler and
+workers to keep totals current. Reads sum cached card and board-description
+counters, never attachment or blob rows, and do not enqueue jobs.
+
+Attachment creation/removal writes a durable accounting record in the same
+transaction. Workers apply only the difference from its last accounted size,
+atomically updating the counter and accounting record. Retried/overlapping jobs
+are safe, failed work remains pending, and batches contain at most 100 records.
+There is no shared account-wide counter lock. Card usage follows its current
+board, including moves made while adjustments are pending. Deleting a card or
+board removes its counters and accounting records.
+
+## Existing installations and bulk imports
+
+After migrating an existing installation, enqueue the initial backfill:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails storage:backfill
+```
+
+Backfill walks attachments in bounded, cursor-based jobs. It is safe to rerun
+and to use while people upload or remove attachments. It does not reset existing
+counters. The regular dispatcher processes the accounting records it creates.
+Normal account imports enqueue an account-scoped backfill automatically; cached
+storage counters are intentionally excluded from exports/imports.
+
+Custom bulk writes that bypass Active Record callbacks must also enqueue a
+backfill after insertion. Bulk deletion of individual attachments bypassing
+callbacks is not supported: use Active Storage's detach/purge APIs instead.
